Tinea Capitis
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Patchy alopecia, Scalp scaling, Black dots/broken hairs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-040
Reason for Visit: Acute development of a highly painful, swollen, pus-filled mass on the scalp associated with severe hair loss and distress in a child.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Parent
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a pediatric patient with rapidly worsening, painful, patchy hair loss and the acute formation of a large, boggy, pus-filled mass on the scalp. The child was in significant distress and unable to sleep. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate a severe staphylococcal abscess requiring immediate surgical drainage from a Kerion (severe inflammatory fungal infection), averting inappropriate surgical intervention and initiating targeted systemic antifungal therapy before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "His head has huge bald patches that are completely crusted over, and today this massive, squishy lump appeared that oozes pus. He's crying constantly from the pain."
History of Present Illness: The 6-year-old patient developed patchy hair loss and fine scaling on the scalp 3 weeks ago, initially resembling severe dandruff. Over the past 7 days, the parent noted prominent thick crusting and intense pruritus of the scalp. The condition has acutely deteriorated over the last 48 hours, with the rapid development of a 4 cm boggy, tender, suppurative inflammatory mass (kerion) on the vertex. The parent reports seeing scattered erythematous pustules at the base of the surrounding hair follicles, and a distinct "black dot" appearance where hair shafts have broken off at the scalp surface. The child is experiencing severe localized pain, preventing sleep or normal play. No fever or systemic illness.
Functional Status: Independent pediatric patient, currently highly distressed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe pain; risk of permanent scarring alopecia if not aggressively treated; highly contagious to other children.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 110 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 21 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, tearful, resisting any examination of the head due to severe tenderness.
Physical Exam: The scalp demonstrates multiple 2-3 cm areas of patchy alopecia with prominent, thick, greyish-white scaling. Within the patches, numerous "black dots" are visible, representing hair shafts broken flush with the epidermis. On the vertex, there is a striking 4x4 cm highly inflammatory, elevated, boggy mass (Kerion). It is exquisitely tender, fiercely erythematous, and studded with multiple follicular pustules actively weeping thick, purulent material upon minimal pressure. There is severely tender, prominent bilateral posterior cervical and occipital lymphadenopathy. No fluctuance typical of a simple unilocular staphylococcal abscess is felt; the mass feels spongy throughout.
Environmental Observations: A family dog (puppy) was recently acquired, a common vector for Microsporum canis.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Tinea Capitis with acute Kerion formation - Status: Acute, highly inflammatory phase
Differentials:

Staphylococcal Abscess (Furuncle/Carbuncle) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of classic patchy alopecia, "black dots", and the widespread thick scaling preceding the mass. A kerion is a spongy, hypersensitivity reaction to fungus, whereas an abscess is a single cavity of pus.

Seborrheic Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of patchy hair loss, broken hairs, and the acute, severe, painful inflammatory mass (kerion), which do not occur in simple seborrheic dermatitis.

Alopecia Areata - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The intense inflammation, boggy purulent mass, severe scaling, and broken hairs. Alopecia areata presents as completely smooth, non-inflamed skin.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to risk of permanent scarring and high communicability)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic triad of Tinea Capitis (patchy alopecia, scaling, broken hairs) culminating in a severe inflammatory Kerion. Triage accurately flagged the painful, purulent scalp mass as requiring urgent assessment. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was absolutely critical to correctly diagnose the mass as a fungal hypersensitivity reaction (Kerion) rather than a simple bacterial abscess. Misdiagnosing this as a staphylococcal abscess would lead to inappropriate, painful surgical incision and drainage, causing unnecessary trauma and failing to treat the underlying dermatophyte. By correctly identifying the Kerion, initiating systemic oral antifungals, and prescribing potent topical steroids to quickly shut down the destructive inflammation, the intervention prevented an inevitable, potentially harmful emergency department visit and safeguarded the hair follicles from permanent scarring.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Griseofulvin (or Terbinafine depending on local pediatric guidelines/availability) to be commenced immediately; systemic therapy is mandatory for Tinea Capitis. Prescribed a short course of oral Prednisolone 1mg/kg daily for 5 days to rapidly suppress the severe inflammatory hypersensitivity reaction (the kerion) to prevent permanent scarring alopecia. Prescribed Ketoconazole 2% shampoo to reduce surface shedding and contagion.
Positive Behavioral Change: By giving your child the oral antifungal medicine faithfully every single day for the next 6-8 weeks with a fatty meal (like a glass of full-cream milk), you will ensure the medicine absorbs fully and successfully kills the fungus deep in the hair roots. By taking the short course of steroids starting tomorrow morning, you will forcefully turn off the massive, painful swelling on his scalp, preventing the hair roots from being permanently destroyed.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3-5 days to monitor the reduction in inflammation, arrange fungal cultures if desired, and confirm the long-term systemic treatment plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness suddenly starts spreading rapidly down the forehead or neck, if the child develops a high fever over 38.5C, or becomes extremely lethargic and confused, present to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Oral antifungal therapy requires many weeks of treatment. Hair regrowth will be slow and is not guaranteed if severe scarring has already occurred.
Clinical Course: The extreme pain, oozing, and swelling of the kerion should improve dramatically within 3-5 days on the oral steroids. The fungal infection itself will take 6-8 weeks of daily pills to cure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to squeeze or "pop" the large lump, as it is a solid sponge of inflammation, not a fluid-filled boil, and squeezing will cause severe pain and scarring.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your child has a severe ringworm infection in the hair roots, likely from the new puppy. The massive, painful lump is actually an extreme allergic reaction by their immune system trying to fight the fungus. Creams cannot reach deep enough; they must take the oral antifungal for weeks to cure the infection, and the oral steroids will quickly stop the painful swelling.
Actionable Steps: Do not share hats, combs, or pillows with other children. Have the new puppy checked by a veterinarian for ringworm.

